## 6. Architecture

### 6.1 Components

**[CHOICE]** Four packages with one-way dependencies:

1. `core` (pure Python, no Qt): project model, schema validation, coordinate algebra, annotation command stack, provenance graph, import/export logic. 100% headless-testable — this is where our regression discipline lives (§4.1 lesson).
2. `access` (pure Python): source adapters over **tifffile** (TIFF/OME-TIFF/ImageJ: page/tile reads, Zarr interface, memmap for contiguous stacks [S14]) and **zarr** (OME-Zarr 0.4 chunk reads); LRU chunk cache; worker threads; progress/cancel events.
3. `viewer`: Qt (PySide6) host + **napari embedded as the canvas** (Qt widget, dims/layer API). Fallback bounded alternative: custom Qt+vispy tiled canvas (§6.5).
4. `app`: windows, panels (metadata inspector showing verbatim + normalized sidecar, provenance graph, export dialogs), keyboard map, accessibility.

**[INFERENCE]** The napari-embed decision is isolated behind the `viewer` interface (set source+map, set view, deliver annotation edits), so swapping to a custom canvas is a bounded change, not a rewrite.

### 6.2 Why napari as viewer, concretely

**[FACT]** napari is built for browsing/annotating large multi-dimensional images on Qt+VisPy (its own release description) [S4]; its dims/transform model matches §5.3 [S5]; it renders points/shapes/labels layers we can bind to our annotation model. **[FACT]** Its gaps we must cover are equally documented: no project persistence (QuPath's role), partial undo only [S6]. **[INFERENCE]** Embedding buys years of rendering engineering (tiled/multiscale, channel compositing, 3D) for a 6-person group; we do not modify napari internals, we drive its public API.

### 6.3 Memory plan (the 3 GB target)

**[VALIDATION-exec W2]** Modeled source: uint16, T=2, Z=200, Y=2048, X=2048 = 3.355 GB. One XY plane = 8 MiB = 0.25% of the source; a 256×256 tile = 128 KiB; a 1 GiB LRU cache holds 128 planes. Receipt `exec-vfni4c27`. **Honest discriminator:** the check *refuted* my initial capacity framing — even 3 full copies (10.07 GB) still fit in 16 GiB (5 copies fit). **[INFERENCE]** The bounded-access rule is therefore adopted not because capacity forces it at exactly 3 GB, but because: (a) *latency* — decoding one plane (8 MiB) must not wait on a 3.4 GB decode; (b) upconversion to float32 for processing doubles every buffer (6.7 GB/copy) and is avoided — samples stay native dtype end-to-end; (c) headroom — cache + viewer + annotation masks + OS should coexist comfortably, and the same design must survive the 20 GB stack the group acquires next year. Design rule: **no full-array materialization on any interactive path**; whole-array reads occur only in explicit, cancelable, progress-reported batch ops (export derived image, mask resample).

### 6.4 Concurrency, cancellation, progress

**[CHOICE]** Read path: bounded thread pool (default 2 I/O + 2 decode workers) issuing tile/plane jobs; every job carries a cancel token; UI shows per-op progress (bytes/stages) and a cancel button that takes effect between tiles (≤128 KiB granularity for tiles, ≤8 MiB for plane decodes). **[FACT]** Mechanism precedent: tifffile exposes threaded read knobs (`ioworkers`, `maxworkers`) and per-tile decode via `page.decode()` on raw offsets [S14].

### 6.5 Bounded alternatives (recorded, not pursued first)

- **Alt-V1: custom Qt+vispy tiled canvas.** More control, less dependency risk; costs the multiscale/3D machinery napari provides. Kept viable by the `viewer` interface boundary.
- **Alt-A2: build on QuPath as a plugin platform (Java).** Reuses project+viewer+persistence outright. Costs: Java stack for a Python-lab team, annotation model fit (pathology-oriented), and license compatibility must be evaluated before any code reuse (license text not captured in this study — **unverified**, must be checked before adoption).
- **Alt-A3: napari plugin only (no standalone persistence).** Rejected for v1: it inherits napari's open undo/persistence gaps [S6] and cannot own the project contract the brief demands.

