## 10. Opportunity and alternative

**Opportunity (adopted) [INFERENCE].** Make **OME-Zarr 0.4 the project's derived-data and exchange format** (derived images, mask layers): it is a captured, stable public spec [S13] with ecosystem readers (napari consumes it; tifffile writes NGFF-conformant zarr [S14]) — the group gets free interop with the tools it already uses (napari, and later OMERO/web viewers), and our export path doubles as an archive path. This turns a storage decision into an interoperability asset rather than a lock-in.

**Plausible alternative (documented, not chosen) [CHOICE].** *Extend QuPath instead of building Slidebench:* the project/persistence/relocation/recovery mechanisms already exist and are battle-tested (§4.2), and it has a mature annotation UI. This is genuinely viable if the group accepts the Java toolchain and QuPath's annotation semantics; blockers to verify first: license compatibility for any code reuse (unverified here), fit of its measurement model to microscopy (vs pathology) workflows, and our float64-µm/NGFF contract — we would need a bridge either way. **[INFERENCE]** For a 6-person Python-centric lab, owning a small core on napari/tifffile is the lower long-run cost; but if the group later adopts digital-pathology workflows, QuPath becomes the stronger base — this is why §5's contract is tool-agnostic (JSON + NGFF + CSV), so the *data* survives a platform switch.

**Deferred ideas** (recorded, not obligations): see `leads.json` — GeoJSON overlay under a documented mapping, pyramid generation, ImageJ/NDTiff read support, macro recording (napari #2579 direction), OMERO/web export, batch annotation QA tooling.

